#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106627} :activity/id #uuid "5207c8f6-7d65-5c9f-8bbb-af66fdcc9b1d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049027 :topic/id #uuid "d4a5e636-5453-5120-957b-34a9e32197ae" :topic/math-academy-id 1403} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107043 :tutorial/content "Remember that longer sides in a triangle are opposite to larger angles, and vice versa. But what if two (or more) sides have the same length?\n\nAs you might have guessed, if two sides have the same length, then the opposite angles have the same measure. And vice-versa: if two angles have the same measure, then the opposite sides have the same length.\n\nSo, we obtain the following important properties of isosceles and equilateral triangles:\n\n- A triangle is **equilateral** if and only if all of its angles are of $60^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/ad/ad56532db83ae54eeabf04d5a182a850bed14d9d0a65518b8048c3c0c112c371.png)\n\n- A triangle is **isosceles** if and only if it has two congruent angles.\n\n![](images/2b/2b5d6779527eae10e108ffcdc80745f63f3f38e41474673990bf74c83d2e6a64.png)\n\n**Note:** The statement above encompasses two theorems:\n\n- The **isosceles triangle theorem**:\n\n*If a triangle is isosceles, then the base angles are congruent.*\n\n- The **converse isosceles triangle theorem**: \n\n*If two angles in a triangle have the same measure, then the triangle must be isosceles.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "87d68609-ccd1-5ebd-b129-2c6993e05b75" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2227 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5440fe87-c485-5fa7-ab27-be4de4746fa1" :step/math-academy-id 164419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105525 :question/id #uuid "df6c46c7-a65c-578c-a9b3-e75de73c527a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2718"} :knowledge-point/i